.TH ATLAS-NC 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-nc \- Open a plain TCP (or UDP) connection and pipe text or files through it: the Swiss army knife for talking to services.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
nc  \-zv  \-w 2  127.0.0.1  8099
 |   |    |     |         '\-\-\- port (or a range 8000\-8100)
 |   |    |     '\-\-\- host
 |   |    '\-\-\- \-w = give up after 2 seconds
 |   '\-\-\- \-z = just check the port is open (send no data), \-v = say so
 '\-\-\- the program (this guide uses the OpenBSD netcat that Ubuntu installs)
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Connect to a CTF service and read its prompt (\fBnc host port\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Check whether a port is open; grab a banner; send a hand\-written HTTP request.
.IP \(bu 2
Move a file between two machines in a lab.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-v\fR
Verbose: report connection results.
.br
Use it when: See \fBsucceeded\fR/\fBrefused\fR.
.TP
\fB\-z\fR
Scan mode: connect and close.
.br
Use it when: Quick port check.
.TP
\fB\-w N\fR
Timeout in seconds.
.br
Use it when: Do not hang.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR
Listen for an incoming connection (OpenBSD: \fBnc \-l HOST PORT\fR).
.br
Use it when: Receive a file, catch a connection in a lab.
.TP
\fB\-N\fR
Shut down the network socket on end of input (OpenBSD).
.br
Use it when: Make a file transfer finish.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR
UDP instead of TCP.
.br
Use it when: DNS\-like services, games.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR
No DNS lookups.
.br
Use it when: Faster, avoids leaking names.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR
Keep listening after one connection.
.br
Use it when: Serve repeatedly.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Check whether a port is open"
\fB\-z\fR connects without sending data. The first port has the lab server; the second has nothing.
.PP
.nf
$ nc \-zv \-w2 127.0.0.1 8099; nc \-zv \-w2 127.0.0.1 9
.fi
.PP
.nf
Connection to 127.0.0.1 8099 port [tcp/*] succeeded!
nc: connect to 127.0.0.1 port 9 (tcp) failed: Connection refused
.fi
.PP
One \fBsucceeded\fR and one \fBrefused\fR. Same idea as an Nmap TCP connect scan for a single port.
.SS "2. Hand\-write an HTTP request"
HTTP is plain text over TCP. \fB\er\en\fR line endings and a blank line end the request.
.PP
.nf
$ printf 'GET /raw HTTP/1.0\er\enX\-A: 1\er\en\er\en' | nc \-w2 127.0.0.1 8099 | head \-8
.fi
.PP
.nf
HTTP/1.0 200 OK
Server: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.12.3
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:22:33 GMT
Content\-Type: text/plain
X\-Demo: echo

GET /raw
X\-A: 1
.fi
.PP
The server replies with a status line, headers and the echoed body: you spoke HTTP with no browser.
.SS "3. Send a message to a listener"
One side listens (\fB\-l\fR) and writes what it receives to a file; the other connects and sends a line.
.PP
.nf
$ (nc \-l 127.0.0.1 9099 > got.txt &) ; sleep 0.5; echo 'hello from the client' | nc \-N 127.0.0.1 9099; sleep 0.5; cat got.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
hello from the client
.fi
.PP
The file contains the message. Two terminals, one connection: the basis of every \fBnc host port\fR challenge.
.SS "4. Transfer a file and verify it"
Redirects (\fB<\fR, \fB>\fR) feed a file into and out of the socket. Compare hashes to prove nothing changed.
.PP
.nf
$ (nc \-l 127.0.0.1 9100 > recv.bin &) ; sleep 0.5; nc \-N 127.0.0.1 9100 < hello; sleep 0.5; cmp hello recv.bin && echo identical; sha256sum hello recv.bin
.fi
.PP
.nf
identical
8feb47265ff2233ef1420033f46617e67605c3234209fbd69d62e9468e3eb228  hello
8feb47265ff2233ef1420033f46617e67605c3234209fbd69d62e9468e3eb228  recv.bin
.fi
.PP
\fBidentical\fR and two matching hashes. Always verify transfers in forensics work.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
There are several netcats: OpenBSD (\fBnc \-l host port\fR), traditional (\fBnc \-l \-p port\fR, has \fB\-e\fR), and \fBncat\fR from Nmap. Check \fBnc \-h\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A connection that hangs often means you did not send the newline the service waits for.
.IP \(bu 2
Without \fB\-N\fR/\fB\-q\fR, a transfer may never exit; add a timeout (\fB\-w\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Only connect to hosts you are allowed to test.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Connect to the lab server and send a PUT with a body using only \fBnc\fR.
.IP 2. 3
Send \fBhello\fR over UDP between two terminals (\fB\-u\fR).
.IP 3. 3
Check which of ports 8095\-8100 are open with \fBnc \-zv\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
ncat, socat, telnet, curl, nmap, pwntools remote().
